    "content": "The software-architect agent has completed a comprehensive analysis of your crawler.js file. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Key Architectural Issues\n\n1. **Monolithic Design**: The WebCrawler class handles too many responsibilities (fetching, parsing, display, storage) - violates Single Responsibility Principle\n\n2. **Limited Scalability**: \n   - No support for multiple URLs or queue management\n   - No rate limiting (risk of getting IP banned)\n   - Synchronous parsing blocks the event loop\n   - All data stored in memory\n\n3. **Weak Error Handling**:\n   - No retry logic for failed requests\n   - No timeout protection\n   - No circuit breaker pattern\n   - Single failures can crash entire crawl\n\n4. **Poor Extensibility**:\n   - Hard to add features like link following, robots.txt compliance\n   - Parsing logic tightly coupled to cheerio\n   - No plugin system\n\n## Top Recommendations\n\n**Immediate (can do now)**:\n- Add fetch timeout (10 min)\n- Implement retry with exponential backoff (30 min)\n- Extract configuration to separate file (20 min)\n\n**Short-term (this week)**:\n- Separate parsing into extractors using Strategy pattern (2 hrs)\n- Add structured logging (1 hr)\n- Implement rate limiting (2 hrs)\n\n**Long-term**:\n- Refactor to Pipeline Architecture with separate stages\n- Add queue-based multi-URL crawling\n- Consider using Crawlee framework for production use\n\nThe analysis includes detailed code examples for each recommendation, trade-off comparisons, and a migration strategy. Would you like me to implement any of these improvements?",
